#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:9
msgid "Using model options in PyBaMM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:11
msgid ""
"In this notebook we show how to pass options to models. This allows users to "
"do things such as include extra physics (e.g. thermal effects) or change the "
"macroscopic dimension of the problem (e.g. change from a 1D model to a 2+1D "
"pouch cell model). To see all of the options currently available in PyBaMM, "
"please take a look at the documentation `here <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/"
"latest/source/api/models/base_models/base_battery_model.html>`__. For more "
"information on combining submodels explicitly to create your own custom "
"model, please see the `Using Submodels notebook <using-submodels.ipynb>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:24
msgid "Example: Solving the SPMe with a lumped thermal model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:26
msgid ""
"PyBaMM is designed to be a flexible modelling package that allows users to "
"easily include different physics within a model without having to start from "
"scratch. In this example, we show how to pass model options to include "
"thermal effects in the SPMe (for more information on the SPMe see `here "
"<SPMe.ipynb>`__). First we import PyBaMM and any other packages we need"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "[ ]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import os\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm\n"
"\n"
"os.chdir(pybamm.__path__[0] + \"/..\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:51
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:57
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:64
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:75
msgid ""
"We then choose out model options, which a set as a dictionary. We choose to "
"model the behaviour in the particle by assuming the concentration profile is "
"quadratic within the particle. We also choose a lumped thermal model (note "
"that this is fully-coupled, i.e. parameters can depend on temperature). For "
"an in-depth look at the thermal models see the `thermal models notebook "
"<thermal-models.ipynb>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"options = {\"particle\": \"quadratic profile\", \"thermal\": \"lumped\"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:95
msgid "We then pass our options to the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "model = pybamm.lithium_ion.SPMe(options)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:116
msgid "We choose to use the parameters from Chen2020."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "param = pybamm.ParameterValues(\"Chen2020\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:137
msgid ""
"We then create and solve a simulation, making sure we pass in our updated "
"parameter values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"simulation = pybamm.Simulation(model, parameter_values=param)\n"
"simulation.solve([0, 3600])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:160
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"&lt;pybamm.solvers.solution.Solution at 0x7f6169de4690&gt;\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:166
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"<pybamm.solvers.solution.Solution at 0x7f6169de4690>\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:173
msgid "<pybamm.solvers.solution.Solution at 0x7f6169de4690>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:184
msgid "Finally we plot the voltage and the cell temperature"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"simulation.plot(\n"
"    [\n"
"        \"Voltage [V]\",\n"
"        \"X-averaged cell temperature [K]\",\n"
"    ]\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/using-model-options_thermal-example.ipynb:211
msgid ""
"<script type=\"application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json\">{\"model_id\": "
"\"449fc9b518774804bf0d4367bb914744\", \"version_major\": 2, "
"\"version_minor\": 0}</script>"
msgstr ""

